import type { CancerInput, TermInput } from "@/lib/schema";
import type { Spike } from "./index";

/**
 * LUNG CANCER: the core layer of the deep dive (25 September 2026), written to the standard of the gallbladder,
 * triple-negative, pancreatic and colorectal files (docs/GALLBLADDER-QA.md, docs/TNBC-QA.md, docs/PANCREATIC-QA.md,
 * docs/COLORECTAL-QA.md). Lung is the highest-burden cancer in the world: 2,637,005 new cases and 1,861,839 deaths a
 * year, first in both lists (GLOBOCAN 2024). This file owns the **taxonomy** of the lung family and the epidemiology,
 * risk, presentation, screening, diagnosis, staging, pathology, prevention and outlook of the two records that carry
 * it. It does not touch treatment, trials, molecular detail or the UK pathway, which other layers own.
 *
 * ===================================================================================================================
 * THE TAXONOMY OF THE LUNG FAMILY (decided here, recorded in docs/CANCER-PAGES.md)
 * ===================================================================================================================
 * The rule (docs/CANCER-PAGES.md): a cancer record is a WHO or PDQ tumour entity and carries a `parent`; a treatment
 * setting is never a record; a cross-cutting group keeps no subtypes; a molecular subgroup is an entity only when a
 * classification names it. Lung is the hardest family in the corpus to fit to that rule because four different
 * classifications are in daily use at once: the WHO Classification of Thoracic Tumours (5th edition, 2021), the NCI
 * PDQ treatment summaries, the clinical small-cell / non-small-cell split that decides the first fork of treatment,
 * and the molecular subsets that decide the second. Five tiers, in this order:
 *
 *   1. ORGAN PARENT.  `lung-cancer` ("Lung cancer (all types)") is the family page. It is an anatomical site record,
 *      not a cross-cutting group like metastatic cancer, so it keeps its `subtypes` strings and gains children. Its
 *      `group` is moved from "thoracic" to "lung" here so the family and the organ system agree: the nine records
 *      left in "thoracic" (mesothelioma and the thymic tumours) are exactly the thoracic cancers that are not lung
 *      cancers, which is the statement the group should make.
 *
 *   2. THE CLINICAL SPLIT.  `nsclc` and `sclc` gain `parent: "lung-cancer"`. Before this file neither had a parent,
 *      so the family strip showed `lung-cancer` with no children and the burden roll-up (scripts/content-gaps.ts,
 *      "Parent-level cancers naming subtypes with no subtype record") counted it as a parent without subtype pages.
 *      Justification under the rule: "non-small-cell lung cancer" is not a WHO 2021 entity (the Blue Book classifies
 *      adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma and the rest directly), but it is a PDQ entity with its own treatment
 *      summary, and the rule accepts "WHO **or** PDQ". Small cell carcinoma is both: a WHO 2021 entity under the lung
 *      neuroendocrine neoplasms and a PDQ summary.
 *
 *   3. HISTOLOGIES.  The ten wave 4 pages (`lung-adenocarcinoma`, `lung-squamous-cell-carcinoma`,
 *      `large-cell-lung-carcinoma`, `pulmonary-sarcomatoid-carcinoma`, `lung-adenosquamous-carcinoma`,
 *      `invasive-mucinous-adenocarcinoma-lung`, `lung-adenocarcinoma-in-situ-and-minimally-invasive`,
 *      `basaloid-squamous-cell-carcinoma-lung`, `lymphoepithelial-carcinoma-lung`, `pulmonary-blastoma`) keep
 *      `parent: "nsclc"`. WHO hangs them off the lung, not off NSCLC, but PDQ hangs them off NSCLC and so does every
 *      clinic: the histology matters because it decides whether a tumour is tested for drivers and which chemotherapy
 *      backbone is safe. Keeping them under `nsclc` also keeps the family depth at three rather than flattening
 *      thirteen records onto one page. They are not re-parented.
 *
 *   4. THE NEUROENDOCRINE SPECTRUM, which crosses the clinical split and is the family's one genuine seam. WHO 2021
 *      puts typical carcinoid, atypical carcinoid, large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma and small cell carcinoma in
 *      one chapter. The corpus splits them, and this file records why: `lung-net` (typical and atypical carcinoid)
 *      stays under `neuroendocrine`, because a reader who arrives at a carcinoid arrives from the neuroendocrine
 *      family and expects the somatostatin-analogue pages beside it; `sclc` sits under `lung-cancer`, because a
 *      reader who arrives at small-cell disease arrives from lung. `lung-lcnec` is created here under `lung-cancer`
 *      rather than under `nsclc` or `sclc`, because it belongs to neither: WHO classifies it as a neuroendocrine
 *      carcinoma beside small cell carcinoma, trials have run it both as an NSCLC and as an SCLC, and no guideline
 *      settles it. Both carcinoid and LCNEC are cross-linked from `lung-cancer` through `related`.
 *
 *   5. MOLECULAR SUBSETS ARE BIOMARKER STATES, NOT ENTITIES.  EGFR-mutant, ALK-rearranged, ROS1, KRAS G12C, MET exon
 *      14, RET, BRAF, HER2, NTRK and NRG1 disease are driver-mutation subsets of lung adenocarcinoma that no
 *      classification separates as tumour types, so under rule 4 of docs/CANCER-PAGES.md they would be strings on the
 *      parent and readouts of kind `biomarker`, not cancer records. Ten of them are cancer records in
 *      `../lung-subtypes.ts`, written before the rule. They are **kept for their URLs and not added to** (rule 2), and
 *      the canonical home of each state is its biomarker readout in `../biomarker-readouts*.ts`. Nine of the ten
 *      already had one; `nrg1-fusion` did not and is written here, so every molecular subset the family names now has
 *      a readout. The same applies to the four stage records in `../lung-subtypes.ts` (`resectable-nsclc`,
 *      `stage-iii-unresectable-nsclc`, `limited-stage-sclc`, `extensive-stage-sclc`): a treatment setting is never a
 *      record, they predate the rule, they keep their URLs, and no new one is created.
 *
 *   MESOTHELIOMA IS NOT IN THE FAMILY. It arises from the pleura, not the lung; WHO gives it its own chapter; its
 *   staging project is separate from the lung one; and NICE NG12 gives it its own referral rule. It keeps
 *   `group: "thoracic"` and no lung parent, and this file does not add it to `lung-cancer`'s subtypes. It shares the
 *   organ drawing (`lung`, "Lungs, pleura and mediastinum") because the drawing is anatomy, not taxonomy. The same
 *   holds for the thymic tumours and for the childhood umbrella `pleuropulmonary-blastoma`, which is linked from the
 *   parent through `related` and through its own subtypes string but is not a subtype of NSCLC or SCLC.
 *
 * WHAT ALREADY EXISTS AND IS LINKED RATHER THAN REWRITTEN.  `./nsclc.ts` owns NSCLC's treatment rows, trials, drivers,
 * history and pipeline; `./sclc.ts` owns the same for small-cell disease; `../lung-subtypes.ts` owns the driver and
 * stage pages; `../cancers-wave4-lung.ts` owns the ten histologies; `./mesothelioma.ts` owns mesothelioma. The
 * glossary already holds `oligometastatic`, `performance-status`, `resectability`, `tnm-staging`, `staging-systems`,
 * `bronchoscopy`, `radiation-pneumonitis`, `ild`, `pleura`, `pleural-effusion`, `pleurodesis`, `mediastinum`, `tps`
 * and `sclc-molecular-subtypes`, and the technologies `low-dose-ct-screening`, `nhs-targeted-lung-health-check`,
 * `smoking-cessation-after-diagnosis` and `optellum-virtual-nodule-clinic`. Those are supplemented, not duplicated.
 *
 * Patches merge in registration order and scalars override, so the `summary` each of `./nsclc.ts` and the base record
 * wrote is carried over verbatim at the top of the new summary (edit it here, not there).
 *
 *   records patched              lung-cancer (taxonomy, epidemiology, risk, screening, staging, prevention, FAQ),
 *                                nsclc (parent, epidemiology, risk, presentation, screening, diagnosis, staging,
 *                                pathology, prevention, outlook, FAQ), sclc (parent and aliases only)
 *   records created              lung-lcnec (WHO 2021 entity, parent lung-cancer), nrg1-fusion (biomarker readout)
 *   terms                        targeted-lung-health-check, pack-year, never-smoker-lung-cancer, ebus-tbna,
 *                                tnm-9-lung-cancer, resectability-lung-cancer, pneumonitis, pulmonary-nodule,
 *                                lung-rads, radon, asbestos, smoking-cessation, haemoptysis, pancoast-tumour,
 *                                spread-through-air-spaces, ttf1-p40, mediastinal-lymph-node-stations
 *   supplements                  nhs-targeted-lung-health-check, low-dose-ct-screening, bronchoscopy, lung-net,
 *                                mesothelioma, pleuropulmonary-blastoma, lung-adenocarcinoma,
 *                                lung-squamous-cell-carcinoma
 *
 * Every figure is quoted from the page or abstract named beside it: the IARC trachea, bronchus and lung fact sheet
 * (GLOBOCAN 2024 estimates), SEER Cancer Stat Facts, Cancer Research UK's statistics and patient pages, NHS pages,
 * NICE NG12, NG122 and NG209, the UK National Screening Committee recommendation, the GOV.UK announcement of the
 * national programme, and papers read through the Europe PMC REST API (cached under /tmp/lung). Nothing is inferred.
 */
